  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "Consider \\(x^2+px+q=0,\\) where \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are positive numbers. If the roots of this equation differ by \\(1,\\) then \\(p\\) equals:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(\\sqrt{4q+1}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(q-1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(-\\sqrt{4q+1}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(q+1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\sqrt{4q-1}\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "a",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1970-p14.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The two roots differ in absolute value by \\[ \\sqrt{p^2-4q}. \\] Hence \\(p^2-4q=1,\\) so \\(p^2=4q+1.\\) Since \\(p\\) is positive, \\[ p=\\sqrt{4q+1}. \\]\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A.",
      "hints": [
        "Use the quadratic formula to express the difference of the roots",
        "The difference has magnitude \\(\\sqrt{p^2-4q}\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
